---
source: crates/togi/tests/read/watch.rs
expression: text(&ran.stdout)
---
Show the session as a read-only dashboard

It redraws when the journal changes and once a second for the clock. During an idle trial it holds still, clock included, until the journal changes, a key is pressed or the terminal is resized. The Main page answers four things: which phase and how far through it, what runs now, which cores need a look (all sixteen gauges, each with its tuning-profile offset, applied offset, recorded solo limit and failure), and what happened recently. Eight more pages hold the details: Cores, Cycle, R7 evidence, Hunts / probes, BIOS profile, History, Journal and Help. On a terminal, the left and right arrows cycle the nine pages, the up and down arrows, `PgUp`, `PgDn`, Home and End scroll, Esc or `m` returns to Main, `?` opens the help, `l` the newest 400 journal events, and `q` exits the dashboard. A journal scrolled back holds its list still and counts new events until End. It is rendered from the journal, which it reloads only when it changes.

The screen's words are explained by `?` on the screen itself; the rules behind them are in docs/spec/tuner.md.

On a terminal it fills the screen until interrupted; otherwise it prints one frame of --width by --height and exits. An unreadable or incompatible journal appears in the frame and on stderr, and one-frame watch exits 1; live watch keeps showing the problem and retries when the journal changes. A journal another build wrote, whose first line is not a togi journal header, is refused before any frame: exit 1 with the cutover step on stderr, live or not. A missing journal (no session yet) is not an error. An event of a kind this build does not know is skipped and an unknown field is ignored; the footer says which. The tuning boot shows it on tty1 with `--tuning-boot`, which keeps it open: `q`, `Q`, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-D do nothing on every page and the help and footer leave the quit key out, so only stopping the service, or a signal such as SIGTERM, ends it. A terminal alone never sets it. `NO_COLOR` turns colour off.

Usage: togi watch [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --state-dir <path>
          State directory
          
          [default: /var/lib/togi]

      --width <columns>
          Frame width in columns, 1 to 1000, when stdout is not a terminal
          
          [default: 240]

      --height <rows>
          Frame height in rows, 1 to 1000, when stdout is not a terminal
          
          [default: 67]

      --tuning-boot
          Run as the unattended tuning-boot dashboard: ignore q, Q, Ctrl-C and Ctrl-D on every page and leave the quit key out of the help and footer

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  togi watch                                 The session in the default state directory, full screen
  togi --state-dir <dir> watch > frame.txt   One 240x67 frame of the session in <dir>

